# The Gift Periyar Gives Us!
Tamil title: பெரியார் நமக்குத் தரும் பரிசு!

## It Is Not in Our Nature to Vilify Him

**Forgers!**  
**Embezzlers!!**

Dear friends, you have now become the targets of these two grave accusations. These are the gifts Periyar gives us this week after our many years of work: people who forge signatures! Cheats! As long as there were people who followed him with their eyes closed, he called them steadfast men, heroes, and Dravidians. We showed even the slightest inclination to use the rationalism he had sharpened for us to examine his own words, and anger arose at once. He now abuses us as forgerers and cheats. What consolation can I offer comrades whose hearts are wounded by this abuse, except to point out that I too have been struck by it and am enduring it?

Branches of the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam are flourishing throughout the country. People known to the country and people who have come forward to perform good work are making these branches prosper in many places. They establish a DMK branch only after properly convening the local Dravidar Kazhagam branch, seeking its opinion, taking a vote, and clearly determining whether there is support either for a resolution to change its name to Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam or for a resolution to dissolve the organization called Dravidar Kazhagam and create a new organization. Periyar disparages and condemns this change of name, conducted by a method anyone could accept, as "forgery."

In many respects he is senior to all of us: in age, means, and strength. When he reviles us, what can we do except endure it? His very reason for reviling us is the expectation that hot-blooded young people will refuse to bear such scorching words and will strike back with abuse; he can then show those speeches to our people, speak persuasively, and seek their support. I ask you to endure the ugly abuse that comes from him not merely to prevent us from falling prey to this cunning plan, but for another genuine reason as well. Even today we have not forgotten the benefits that both we and our country have received. Consequently, we have not acquired the base quality of vilifying him, however hard he keeps prodding us to provoke it. We must never acquire it.

The country sees him condemning us without cause and sees us enduring those condemnations. The country will deliver a just verdict.

When he calls the renaming involved in establishing local branches "forgery" and "fraud," the people of each town, the agents of those who govern it, and neutral observers are all watching the proceedings closely. They know whether what we have undertaken is honest action or fraud.

He also says that legal cases may somehow arise. If they do, we shall have to attend to those cases too. What else can we do? Let them come. Once circumstances have forced us to part from him, can any of the other hardships be impossible to endure? The quality that says, "However many hardships surround us, our effort shall endure as long as we endure," has not withered within us. When honesty resides in our hearts and purity in the action we have undertaken, how can there be room to worry that hardship may come?

Periyar has adopted one great misconception and is suffering severely as a result. He supposes that I took immense pains, devised cunning plans, sent friends as emissaries throughout the land, and constructed an opposition front. It is true that an opposition front has formed, and true that it now flourishes as a separate organization. But he is wrong to imagine that I spent all my ingenuity upon creating this situation day and night. Naturally, many further errors sprout from that false belief.

Circumstance created me; vilification is making me grow.

I did not create the circumstances, and vilification is not an occupation familiar to me.

Before division and difference arose in the Dravidar Kazhagam, many comrades who are angry today took pleasure in inviting me and pride in welcoming and honouring me. Even in those days their complaint against me was that I was indifferent and inaccessible, that I came once only after being troubled ten times. When Needamangalam Arumugam, the actor M. R. Radha, Nattarampalli Venkataraman, and many other friends wished to offer me garlands and joy, affection and support, I was one who almost always slipped away. To imagine that a man of such a disposition plotted from moment to moment, counted the people in every town, and planned an opposition front is to have known me without understanding me properly.

The circumstances naturally created by Periyar's proposed marriage caused the opposition front to form, not I.

One may be tempted to think that the situation arose because I wrote about the marriage. That too was not the cause. Even when I wrote, the statement did not take shape as my individual opinion or propaganda. It emerged as the collected thought of the many comrades whose hearts seethed over Periyar's marriage arrangement. In other words, by the time I published that statement, the opposition front had already formed. I did not form it. The front was not created by my deceit or villainy, my cunning or ability, tireless work or seductive plan. It formed naturally and democratically. That is precisely why the front possesses living force.

Only by recognizing this can the situation be assessed as it truly is.

This is where Periyar and his supporters adopt their mistaken view. They think that I stubbornly carried on malicious propaganda, deluded and ruined many people, and thereby brought an opposition front into being. Wrong! Wrong! A great wrong! The situation developed naturally and democratically.

Could a single statement from me delude so many comrades? If I possessed such power, the statement I issued in August should also have created an opposition front. Did anything of the kind occur? No. I knew it would not. That is why I wrote in that very statement that disciplinary action could be taken against me if desired. When the August statement appeared, did Asaithambi, Vallimuthu, Chitrarasu, Srinivasan, and countless other comrades say, "Anna has written it, therefore it alone is right"? Our comrades are not so lacking in firmness. They stated with dignity that they could not accept my opinion.

Should we not consider why the support denied to my August statement was given to the view I expressed during the marriage controversy? Thoughtful consideration makes the answer clear: the opposition front was neither the product of my beguiling words nor the victory of a selfish plan. It was a product of circumstance, a bud of democracy.

I therefore ask his supporters not to make room in their minds for a false idea and thereby allow many more false ideas to sprout.

Once an error is accepted at the foundation, further errors grow rapidly. That is how the tendency on that side has reached the point of hurling abuse about "forgery" and "fraud."

Because they wrongly imagine that my cunning and selfishness created this new organization, they suppose that the way to disrupt it is to revile and discredit me. They have made me into an object of research. They examine my situation, thoughts, and actions and say, "He knew even then. Is this alone not proof? Was he not the man who did all these things?"

This mistaken method cannot destroy the new organization called the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam. If I alone had caused it to form, reviling me might destroy the organization. But this organization is a product of circumstance, the voice of countless comrades' hearts, and a bud of democracy. It cannot be abolished by disparaging and condemning me.

The quality of this organization is not founded upon my ability alone. It is built upon the abilities of innumerable Dravidian comrades. I do not rejoicefully claim that the Dravidar Kazhagam exists solely through the ability and labour of Periyar as one man, nor will I ever say such a thing, for it would not be true. Even thus far, others have performed and continue to perform more of the difficult labour needed to establish the DMK than I have. I say so with pride and gladness and offer my thanks to those devoted workers.

Until today, letters telling me, "This must be done; it must be arranged in this way," have continuously arrived from those dear friends and have given me enthusiasm and feeling. I, however, have done nothing by methods such as recruiting people, testing the depths, or sending emissaries. I have no habit of it and no training in it. That is why I say that reviling me cannot destroy the DMK.

The North Arcot District Dravidar Kazhagam became the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam as a result of a meeting held at Tiruvathipuram, twelve miles from Kanchipuram. Comrade Subbarayan of Tiruvathipuram led the effort. I had been in Kanchipuram for five days, and friend Subbarayan was very dear to me. Tiruvathipuram was only twelve miles away. Yet had I met him to discuss this matter or conveyed an opinion even by letter? No. He was a dear friend nearby, yet I neither saw him nor sent a letter directing how the matter should proceed. Why? He bears even greater responsibility and concern for this issue than I do. I trusted that he would do what was right and possessed the ability needed to do it. Events occurred exactly as I trusted they would.

I explain this because every action taken thus far for the DMK has occurred like the action at Tiruvathipuram: without prompting or incense from me, naturally and democratically. It is not the victory of one individual, a selfish man or a traitor. It is the achievement of the countless Dravidian comrades whose hearts were inflamed. Is reviling me the way to destroy this achievement? No! No! There could be no better way to make me grow.

Contrary to what he thinks and says, I did not plan and manufacture the present situation, nor was it my long-standing desire.

Had the Dravidar Kazhagam been organized as a political party seeking to capture power and ascend the seat of government, there might at least have been room to accuse me or another person of selfishly plotting to seize the Kazhagam and obtain office. But the Dravidar Kazhagam is not an instrument for hunting immediate political gain. I have always supported that position, and I remain of the same view. Under these circumstances, what need could there be for selfish ambition?

Does Periyar imagine that if he endlessly calls us selfish men and traitors, the people will believe him exactly as he says? Will they never reflect and ask, "What selfish interest can be found here? Whom has he betrayed?"

I understand clearly that, even after this enormous storm, Periyar speaks in this fashion only to encourage those who remain with him and protect their confidence from breaking. That is why I feel neither anger nor grief.

If you understand this subtle point, you too will feel neither anger nor grief when he utters scorching words. I explain it for that reason.

Besides accusing me of having planned my work and deluded the people, he hints that I had harboured treacherous thoughts for three years. He has apparently said this from time to time! If he knew three years ago that I was a traitor, why did he honour me only a few months ago at Erode by saying that he would hand me the "key to the strongbox"? Did a traitor deserve the presidency of a special conference, a gift, a bribe, or a hint that he would be given a share? What can this mean?

Had he given me even the slightest hint through word or deed from which I could understand the charge of "traitor" he makes today, I would not have laid my head to sleep even in the direction where he lived. I would have said, "A great man who must work for the liberation of Dravidian society should not also be burdened with the task of eliminating a traitor. Let him be spared that," and left at once.

I have never been accustomed to asking for space or knocking upon a door. Even now, when a railway carriage is crowded, I simply remain standing; I do not force my way inside. Had I received the faintest indication that the leader of the Kazhagam suspected me and regarded me as a traitor, I would have thanked him for the affection and support he had shown until then and departed.

As I have explained many times, this vast world has room even for ordinary people like me. Are there not mendicants who live without the support of any monastery? I would have lived like them. But he gave not the slightest hint. He has condemned me as indolent and said that I roam about with young men, but he never called me a traitor. I never had reason even to imagine that such a thought might have existed in his heart.

The affection and respect he showed me have often made me feel embarrassed. I remember clearly the first time he invited me to Erode and explained the distinction of entering public life. When I later set out to return home, his large carriage was harnessed and I sat inside. As it departed, he walked behind it for the distance of ten houses while I, embarrassed inside, pleaded, "Stop, please do not come any farther, stop!" Those scenes remain with me. From that day until the marriage issue assumed its fearsome form, his conduct toward me never gave me cause to wonder whether he held any low or harmful opinion of me.

He has been angry with me many times, and I too have become angry. Many years ago, while *Viduthalai* was being published from Erode, I became angry and returned to my town. I still possess separate letters written by Periyar and his elder brother to console me and show their affection. When Periyar now places me upon his list of "traitors," I look at those letters and find peace. Truly, I feel happy. Though in today's anger and disappointment he reviles me as a traitor and a selfish man, it gives me joy to remember the attachment and affection he showed me in earlier days.

Each of you will have similar experience, for he once spoke of us all with such pride. I therefore ask you not to attach importance to his abuse today.

An unexpected situation has arisen, compelling him to use language contrary to his own nature. That is all. We must continue our work and, through its performance, make our goodwill known to the country. That is our duty.

I stress again and again that we must neither become angry nor grow weary upon hearing his angry words.

His supporters conduct cunning propaganda. Our new Kazhagam demonstrates only that our cooperation and collective labour are no longer available to the Dravidar Kazhagam. We are not so deranged as to say that Periyar will be unable to travel, that no meetings will occur, or that no one anywhere will join him. Even after thirty years of Self-Respect propaganda, do not Kripananda Variyars still travel here and there delivering Puranic discourses? Our former friend K. M. Balasubramaniam has forgotten Ingersoll, embraced Manikkavasagar, and continues preaching Saivism, at Aduthurai on the sixth, Papanasam on the seventh, and Kottaiyur on the eighth!

When all of them can accomplish so much, is it astonishing that a leader who has performed public work for thirty years, who by his own account serves that cause full-time, who has had people like us as runners in front and beaters of his drum, and who possesses press power, money power, friendship networks, and facilities unequalled by anyone else in the movement should have opportunities to campaign throughout the country?

His supporters point to his tour and sing, "Look! Look! See what force!" Is this truly a way to magnify him? Those comrades who exult must reflect. What momentous tasks he ought to accomplish! How many great questions stand before him! This is the time to attend to the Brahmin and non-Brahmin question, which has developed to a point where an orderly settlement seems possible within our generation. This is the time to consult the leaders of Andhra and Kerala and the organizations of Karnataka and formulate a plan for a Dravidian federation. This is the time to meet Ceylonese political leaders and show a good path to the Tamils being ruined in Ceylon. What immense questions confront him!

After forgetting all these, is the action we expect from such a great leader to go to Arur and nearby places and proclaim, "I possess the power to eliminate those who oppose me, and I have friends there who will support me"? Should his strength and the support available to him be employed only to eliminate me and abuse those who dared join me? That strength ought to suppress Acharya's propaganda for varnashrama, curb Patel's fascist course, and break the arrogance of North Indian imperialists.

That is where the power he accumulated through years of ceaseless travel, without distinguishing village from hamlet or day from night, should be used. Must it finally be employed merely to test whether it is sufficient to eliminate me? Even I find it painful to see his present course. It resembles sharpening and testing a sword meant for the battlefield and then using it to cut down a banana plant growing in the home garden.

Let this course cease. Its target should not be ordinary people like us. It should strike Sardar Patel, Pandit Nehru, Acharya, Bajaj and Birla, Sankara Tambiran, repressive laws, and the rulers' ever-new schemes. I remind Periyar that his strength exists for those purposes.

The reason his position remains merely that of a leader within the province, and even there only one who moves among outsiders, is this very course: he repeatedly directs his strength against people like me and brings needless exhaustion upon himself. Even today he regards all those incidents as "victories." No! No! They were occasions upon which he wasted his power. Anyone who analyses them can understand this well.

He has repeatedly used his strength to prevent democracy from budding within the Kazhagam. "What became of the man who opposed me? What became of this other man who resisted me?" He has spent many years and his accumulated strength taking pride in asking these questions. In the end, what purpose did that strength serve? Merely the satisfaction of declaring, "I possess the power to eliminate anyone who refuses to heed my word"!

He is more than seventy years old, has performed more propaganda work than any leader, and has received a company of comrades in a fashion granted to no other leader. Yet what does he celebrate? "In my realm, I alone am the dictator!" Is that all? Was all that power meant only for this?

If he spends all his time and strength watching who raises a head in the Kazhagam and how each person grows, where will he find time to attend to enormous problems, and how can the thought arise? What is Chitrarasu doing? Where did Mathiazhagan speak? Why was Karunanidhi invited? Why do they support Annadurai? How did Lingam enter the intermediate class? How did Manimozhiyar obtain a newspaper? Why was the women's conference convened?

What does a man who ought to consult the leaders concerned with great questions and act upon them do with his position, facilities, and strength? He engages in "statesmanship" of this kind: "Arrange a meeting in your town. Consult these particular people and obtain their signatures. If a DMK branch is established, immediately issue a denial declaring it invalid."

I am not a fool who imagines that I possess strength equal to his. He must have visited every important town ten times for propaganda; there are more towns I have never visited. It is only natural that I lack his extent of contacts and friendships. Yet within my own capacity I entrust many tasks to friends and ask them to carry the work out, with confidence. Even today Periyar has acquired no such confidence in anything or anyone. Without even worrying that the listener may be hurt, he says, "I believe I must do everything myself. No one can be trusted." What suspicion! What fear! Why this condition?

Even at a moment of crisis marked by the scent of war in Kashmir, unrest in Tibet, war in Burma, and hostility with Pakistan, Pandit Nehru has gone to America and will take a month to return. Did he wonder, "What will become of the country without me? Whom can I trust with such immense responsibility?" He trusts that Patel will attend to his work and Matthai will look after matters. Our Periyar has not a drop of that trust.

An incident comes to mind. A Dravidian students' training school was to be held at Mavur, and its announcement appeared in *Viduthalai* two or three times. The Udumalai case then arose. A statement immediately appeared saying that, because Periyar might have to go to prison, the students' training school had been postponed. What attitude is this? Could the work of training student propagandists not be entrusted to anyone else? What an expansive outlook! Nehru did not think like this. Only our Periyar thinks so.

It is because permanent distrust, suspicion, and fear have taken hold of his thought that he can so easily regard and describe even people like me, who have never raised our voices before him, as traitors. Beyond a certain age and degree of experience, one must not retain the belief that one must personally supervise every task and that nobody else should do any work. The practice of preparing many others and using them to accomplish tasks while watching over and rejoicing in their work must develop. If it does not, the heart inevitably becomes a checkpoint of suspicion. That is why he reviles us all.

I ask you to examine and understand this. Unlike him, let us live by trusting one another. Let us act upon the fundamental truth that everyone possesses the ability to perform some task. Let us nurture the principle that the combined power of many is preferable to the power of one individual. Let him cherish the belief that work can proceed properly only when every authority rests in one place and one hand. We believe that each of us has a responsibility and share in the work, that thought has a task, and that decisions must be made through consultation. In other words, let us work with complete faith in democracy.

We must forget petty quarrels and problems and make useful service to the country our aim. I invite you to work peacefully but eagerly: laugh when suspected, endure when reviled, bear sorrow when it arrives, and take the blow when struck. Thousands of comrades accustomed to labour fill our camp. Blame cannot destroy us; insulting words cannot disrupt our effort.

We want not a trace of the stain of mind that forgets yesterday's affection because of today's dispute. A chariot of victory cannot be driven by harnessing the horse of hatred. Come: let us harness the steeds of enthusiasm, hold them with the reins of goodwill and forbearance, and drive the chariot called the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam along the road called dignity. I invite you because I know your enthusiasm and ability well.

Annadurai

(*Dravida Nadu*, 16 October 1949)

---NOTES---
The article was written during the 1949 split following Periyar's proposed marriage to Maniammai and the formation of the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam. The translation preserves Anna's immediate political argument and does not retrofit later institutional developments into it.

`போர்ஜரி` is the period Tamil transliteration of English "forgery." The opening accusation concerns alleged false signatures or fraudulent renaming of local Dravidar Kazhagam branches.

`பெட்டிச்சாவி` literally means "the key to the strongbox." In context it refers to Periyar's public suggestion at Erode that Anna would be entrusted with future responsibility or succession; the metaphor is retained rather than reduced to a formal office.

`ஆதீனம்` is a Saivite monastic institution, while `பண்டாரம்` can denote an ascetic or mendicant associated with such an institution. Anna uses the comparison humorously to say that even an ordinary person without institutional patronage can find a place in the world.

The contrast between Ingersoll and Manikkavasagar presents K. M. Balasubramaniam as having moved from rationalist thought to Saivite devotion. The spellings and rhetorical characterization follow the source.

`பார்! பார்! என்ன ஜோர் பார்!` is a Hindi-influenced campaign cry meaning approximately "Look, look, see what force!" Its performative flavour is retained in the translation.

The names, locations, organizations, and contemporary references, including Tiruvathipuram, Mavur, the Udumalai case, Ceylon, and Matthai, are rendered conservatively from the source. Where the article offers a political characterization, it remains Anna's characterization rather than an independent historical judgment.

The corrected Tamil source spans twenty-one continuous scans. Its scan-backed OCR repairs, including the title, article number, damaged page openings, organization names, and recurring malformed words, are documented separately in `ocr_concerns.csv`.
